const nnCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · The neuron: weighted sum + squish', note: 'Inputs × weights, summed, passed through an activation function (ReLU, sigmoid). One neuron draws a line; a network of them draws any shape.' },
    { math: '2 · Layers compose simplicity', note: 'Stack neurons in layers: early layers learn edges, deeper layers combine them into patterns. Each layer is a linear map plus nonlinearity — the nonlinearity is essential (without it, layers collapse into one).' },
    { math: '3 · Forward pass and loss', note: 'Data flows through, the output is compared to truth via a loss function (the "wrongness score" from the gradient-descent lesson).' },
    { math: '4 · Backpropagation: blame assignment', note: 'The chain rule from calculus distributes blame for the loss back through every weight. Each weight nudges opposite its gradient. Repeat over millions of examples — training is that loop, nothing more mystical.' },
    { math: '5 · The XOR triumph', note: '1969: Minsky & Papert proved single neurons can\'t compute XOR, and funding froze ("AI winter"). Multilayer networks solve it trivially — and backprop (1986) made training them practical. The winter-to-revolution arc is AI history in miniature.' },
  ],
};

const nnQuizCfg = {
  questions: [
    { q: 'Why is the activation function\'s nonlinearity essential?', choices: ['it speeds up computation', 'without it, stacked layers collapse into a single linear map', 'it prevents division by zero', 'it makes outputs positive'], answer: 1, why: 'A composition of linear maps is still linear — depth would buy nothing. The squish between layers is what lets depth buy genuine expressive power.' },
    { q: 'Backpropagation is best described as…', choices: ['a special optimizer', 'the chain rule, organised to assign blame to every weight efficiently', 'a way to generate data', 'an activation function'], answer: 1, why: 'The loss depends on outputs, which depend on late weights, which depend on early ones — the chain rule propagates that dependence backwards, one reusable computation per layer.' },
    { q: 'The 1969 XOR result froze the field because it showed…', choices: ['neural nets were too slow', 'single-layer perceptrons cannot compute even simple non-linearly-separable functions', 'XOR is impossible to compute', 'computers were too expensive'], answer: 1, why: 'A single neuron draws one straight boundary; XOR needs two. The fix (hidden layers) was known but not trainable practically until backprop (1986) — hence the long winter.' },
    { q: 'The field\'s favourite sanity check — overfit one batch — works because…', choices: ['small data is easier', 'a working network must be able to memorise a handful of examples; failure means a bug', 'overfitting is good', 'it trains faster'], answer: 1, why: 'Memorising 100 examples is nearly the easiest possible task. A network that cannot do it has a broken gradient, a bad learning rate, or a wiring bug — find that before any tuning.' },
  ],
};

